1
00:00:01,035 --> 00:00:05,586
This is Control Center, a governed AI assisted delivery pipeline.

2
00:00:05,621 --> 00:00:07,327
Let us walk it end to end.

3
00:00:08,845 --> 00:00:11,190
Here is what really happens on a project.

4
00:00:11,306 --> 00:00:13,466
One story, three developers.

5
00:00:13,512 --> 00:00:17,471
The first pastes the ticket into their AI assistant.

6
00:00:17,494 --> 00:00:20,420
The second reads a README from two years ago.

7
00:00:20,466 --> 00:00:22,359
The third has only the title.

8
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:24,774
Same story, three different contexts.

9
00:00:24,867 --> 00:00:26,573
So three different answers.

10
00:00:29,145 --> 00:00:34,044
Now the same requirement goes in once, and out comes a set of files.

11
00:00:34,079 --> 00:00:34,799
The epic.

12
00:00:34,845 --> 00:00:36,064
Four user stories.

13
00:00:36,122 --> 00:00:42,775
Twelve acceptance criteria, each a numbered, testable sentence about what done actually means.

14
00:00:42,845 --> 00:00:43,808
The architecture.

15
00:00:43,866 --> 00:00:46,757
And one failing test for every criterion.

16
00:00:46,792 --> 00:00:51,332
All three developers now read the same pack, and all three get it right.

17
00:00:52,835 --> 00:00:54,971
Underneath, it is plain Python.

18
00:00:55,064 --> 00:01:01,937
Four layers: the rules every call reads, the skills for each role, the workflow engine, and the app itself.

19
00:01:01,972 --> 00:01:11,051
Every model call goes through a single module, so the same code can run live, record itself, or replay from disk with no network at all.

20
00:01:14,146 --> 00:01:16,643
The work splits into two halves.

21
00:01:16,701 --> 00:01:19,429
The first half is upstream: agree what done means.

22
00:01:19,452 --> 00:01:24,862
The requirement is read, the design is drawn, the plan is written, and a named human signs it.

23
00:01:24,897 --> 00:01:27,997
Every step leaves a file behind, not a conversation.

24
00:01:33,435 --> 00:01:35,246
Now the real screens.

25
00:01:35,327 --> 00:01:36,163
Intake first.

26
00:01:36,244 --> 00:01:48,029
The business owner uploads the requirement, and the system reads it against the actual code in the target repository, so the analysis is grounded in real files rather than guesswork.

27
00:01:48,063 --> 00:01:51,465
It asks the business its own clarifying questions.

28
00:01:51,488 --> 00:01:57,700
The design step draws the data flow and the entity relationships from the run's own stories.

29
00:01:57,816 --> 00:02:08,671
Then planning turns the epic into user stories, each with acceptance criteria, an owning team, and an estimate, and a dependency map shows which story blocks which.

30
00:02:08,787 --> 00:02:10,192
Finally, Gate One.

31
00:02:10,250 --> 00:02:14,987
Every condition on that checklist must be complete, and a named human signs it.

32
00:02:15,022 --> 00:02:19,259
Until that signature exists, nothing downstream exists either.

33
00:02:24,735 --> 00:02:28,473
This is the developer's loop the part most demos skip.

34
00:02:28,508 --> 00:02:31,213
The developer says, start working on a story.

35
00:02:31,236 --> 00:02:37,320
The agent creates the branch, lists the criteria in build order, copies in the failing tests, and stops.

36
00:02:37,436 --> 00:02:39,155
Nothing planned, nothing written.

37
00:02:39,248 --> 00:02:41,152
Then: plan the next criterion.

38
00:02:41,210 --> 00:02:42,661
It plans exactly one.

39
00:02:42,707 --> 00:02:47,258
The test that proves it, the functions it touches, and how you will see it working.

40
00:02:47,293 --> 00:02:52,076
Then the developer edits that plan, and the agent builds what the file now says.

41
00:02:52,123 --> 00:02:56,674
After three failed attempts it stops and asks, instead of guessing again.

42
00:02:59,046 --> 00:03:03,574
The second half is downstream: build until it is provably done.

43
00:03:03,609 --> 00:03:06,047
The pack lands on a fresh branch.

44
00:03:06,070 --> 00:03:09,472
The developer's agent takes one criterion at a time.

45
00:03:09,797 --> 00:03:11,341
Real tests run.

46
00:03:11,516 --> 00:03:16,891
A second model reviews the work before any human is asked to look at it.

47
00:03:18,505 --> 00:03:19,666
Downstream now.

48
00:03:19,782 --> 00:03:27,386
The delivery pack is published to a fresh branch that only touches managed paths, so it cannot overwrite the team's own files.

49
00:03:27,421 --> 00:03:34,713
Continuous integration runs for real, and each test result is joined back to the acceptance criterion it proves.

50
00:03:34,736 --> 00:03:42,468
If the build itself fails, the test counts read null, never zero, because a build that never compiled is not a passing test run.

51
00:03:42,503 --> 00:03:46,995
Then independent review: a second model checks the work before a human is asked.

52
00:03:47,042 --> 00:03:50,827
And release needs five named approvals, each under its own role.

53
00:03:56,805 --> 00:03:59,951
No phase signs off its own work.

54
00:03:59,986 --> 00:04:11,375
The reviewer is a different model with its own instructions, and its verdict is recorded on the artifact itself, so the next step can refuse to run on work that was never checked.

55
00:04:14,105 --> 00:04:15,997
Every artifact says what made it.

56
00:04:16,090 --> 00:04:19,260
Live AI, rule based, simulated, or human.

57
00:04:19,294 --> 00:04:28,141
A deterministic script is never dressed up as AI, and simulated work is never counted as AI work anywhere.

58
00:04:29,765 --> 00:04:30,891
And it runs.

59
00:04:30,937 --> 00:04:37,706
One requirement went from intake to a released, running application in a single overnight run.

60
00:04:37,857 --> 00:04:43,604
Real repository, real pull request, merged, 98.5% coverage.

61
00:04:45,095 --> 00:04:51,631
The scorecard computes velocity, cycle time, and first time right from the run's own ledgers.

62
00:04:51,747 --> 00:04:55,172
The rest read not measurable, with the reason given.

63
00:04:55,254 --> 00:04:57,877
We could have put a number in every row.

64
00:04:58,052 --> 00:05:01,860
That is exactly why the ones that are there can be trusted.

65
00:05:04,395 --> 00:05:06,218
Governance is the product.

66
00:05:06,310 --> 00:05:11,198
Not how much the AI writes, but how many places a human has to sign.

